/* One coherent informational-reading unit; no invented learner records or outcomes. */
(() => {
  'use strict';
  const D=window.CALTPA,M=D.msWorkedModels['ms-literacy']={};
  const P='Proposed planning response',C='Conditional planning example',R='Actual records required',T='Historical example analysis';
  const m=(id,meaning,example,why,evidence,status=P)=>M[id]={meaning,example,why,evidence,status};
  m('A-group-age','Identify your actual teaching group.',
    'The instructional example is designed for Grade 3. Before teaching it, I need the actual ages and grade level of the selected group and must judge whether the texts, word sequence, and discussion demands fit their learning. No roster or age range has been supplied. The 27 students in the mathematics story do not describe this literacy group.',
    'It distinguishes a designed grade level from verified classroom context.','Actual group ages and grade level from authorized records.',R);
  m('A-group-assets','Explain how documented strengths and interests will inform the unit.',
    'Before selecting the beaver texts, I would ask about students’ questions and experiences with animals, changes to places, and informational reading. I would also examine strengths in explaining, drawing, reading, and using more than one language. If an actual observation shows that learners use diagrams to explain cause and effect, I can build on that resource when connecting a dam to a change in water flow. I cannot assume an interest merely because I chose the topic.',
    'An asset is linked to a teaching decision rather than an invented preference.','A dated conversation, observation, or work sample and its instructional consequence.',C);
  m('A-group-literacy','Separate the components of literacy performance.',
    'I would examine a brief independent word-reading sample, an informational main-idea response, and a discussion sample. I would record whether a difficulty concerns recognizing print, understanding word meaning, linking details, or explaining a reason. If comprehension is stronger after a read-aloud, I would report that condition rather than describe it as independent reading. These records would determine the final text support and word-analysis sequence.',
    'A component-level description can explain the instructional need; worksheet completion cannot.','Actual tasks, dates, responses, and assistance across reading and language performance.',R);
  m('A-group-english','Use verified identification and assessment information appropriately.',
    'I will use authorized school information to describe identified English learners in my actual group. Dated ELPAC information can provide background, but recent explanations and discussion reveal how students use language for this task. I will not convert a Summative overall level directly into a fixed sentence frame or assign the beaver unit’s learners a proficiency profile that was never supplied.',
    'It separates background identification from the language demands of explaining a text.','Identification records, assessment date/grade, relevant domains, and recent task evidence.',R);
  m('A-group-needs','Describe relevant identified needs and required supports.',
    'I will confirm the group’s relevant IEP, 504, and MTSS information through authorized channels and identify supports that affect reading, communication, or participation in this unit. The explanation should connect a support to access or learning, not treat a diagnosis as a complete description of a reader. No counts or individual plans are supplied in this example.',
    'The response must be accurate and instructionally relevant without unnecessary private detail.','Verified counts and relevant supports for the actual group.',R);
  m('A-group-language','Name the program and actual instructional language conditions.',
    'This proposed unit uses English informational texts and English explanation goals. Appropriate multilingual notes or rehearsal may help learners construct meaning before explaining it in the target instructional language. My actual Part A must identify the program and languages used. If teaching in Spanish, I would use the applicable standards and actual instructional setting rather than simply relabel this English example.',
    'It specifies conditions instead of assuming language ability from a home-language label.','Actual program model and instructional languages.',C);
  m('A-group-staff','Explain what any assisting adult will actually do.',
    'If an authorized supporting adult is present, I would clarify whether the role is interpreting, providing a required access support, assisting a small group, or operating approved recording equipment. I would specify when assistance is provided and how it will be recorded during assessment. If no staff member assists, I would state that rather than invent a co-teacher or paraprofessional.',
    'Staff support changes what students experience and how their performance can be interpreted.','Actual personnel, agreed roles, and assistance conditions.',C);
  m('A-FS-age','Identify the selected focus student accurately.',
    'Use the actual focus student’s age and grade and a de-identified label. The current unit is a Grade 3 design, but it does not supply an individual’s age or establish that an older word-analysis sample’s student is the same learner. Select the FS with your program and the current guide’s directions.',
    'It prevents the example’s setting from turning into a fabricated profile.','Authorized age/grade and appropriate FS selection.',R);
  m('A-FS-assets','Describe an actual strength you will use in instruction.',
    'If the FS has demonstrated an ability to explain relationships orally, I would use a brief oral rehearsal before the written main-idea explanation. If the actual strength is word analysis, drawing, or another language resource, I would identify that evidence and a different use. The beaver topic itself is not proof of the FS’s interest in animals.',
    'The asset has a clear role in the planned learning.','A specific FS response or observation showing the asset.',C);
  m('A-FS-literacy','Describe this reader’s skills under identified conditions.',
    'For the selected FS, I need separate evidence of word reading, meaningful word-part analysis, informational comprehension, and explanation. I would record the exact word or passage, the response, and whether it was independent, prompted, or read aloud. A correct definition after teacher reading cannot establish independent decoding, and a fluent oral reading cannot by itself establish comprehension.',
    'The description supports a targeted adaptation instead of a global “low reader” label.','Actual FS reading, meaning, comprehension, and language samples.',R);
  m('A-FS-needs','Identify a literacy need and the language needed to address it.',
    'If recent work shows an accurate detail but a main idea that is only a topic, I would target the relationship between details and a general statement. The related language demand is explaining how a detail supports an idea, not merely using the word because. If the actual difficulty is decoding the passage, I would plan print-reading instruction as well rather than treating every difficulty as a comprehension strategy problem.',
    'The conditional comparison shows how different evidence leads to different instruction.','Recent FS response, identified literacy relationship, and language performance.',C);
  m('A-FS-primary','When this conditional prompt applies, describe primary-language literacy—not English proficiency.',
    'The historical word-analysis sample includes English-language information in a response about primary-language literacy. That does not establish what the learner can read or write in the primary language. For my FS I would use relevant records and appropriate observations, describe what is known, and name what is not yet known. I would not infer primary-language literacy from a home-language designation or an English proficiency label.',
    'It repairs a specific mismatch between the question and the evidence.','Actual primary-language literacy information, if the prompt applies; do not invent it.',T);
  m('A-FS-eld','When applicable, describe the FS’s current English-development needs in this task.',
    'I would compare the FS’s reading, listening, speaking, and writing when explaining a text. If the student accurately identifies a detail but cannot make the relationship clear to a listener, I would target a precise explanation and a relevant response. Dated language-assessment information can inform the support level, but it cannot substitute for this current performance or establish the cause of every reading difficulty.',
    'The ELD need is connected to the unit’s intellectual work.','Relevant English-development records and a recent task-specific explanation.',C);

  m('B-scope-1','Use previous assessments to explain why this unit and its supports are appropriate.',
    'Before finalizing the unit, I will compare independent word reading, word-part meaning, an informational main-idea response, and oral discussion. If the evidence shows that students can name details but do not connect them to a main idea, that will justify explicit modeling of that relationship. If print reading limits access, I will select appropriate sound–spelling instruction and document read-aloud support separately. The actual findings, dates, and responses belong in my submitted plan.',
    'It describes an assessment-to-instruction decision rather than claiming undocumented readiness.','Recent actual assessment findings; this proposed unit supplies no baseline results.',C);
  m('B-scope-2','List only standards that the planned instruction and assessment genuinely address.',
    'The primary meaning-making anchors are RI.3.1 and RI.3.2: text-based answers and a main idea supported by details. RI.3.6 informs comparing the author’s viewpoint with the reader’s; it is not a verbatim fact/opinion sorting standard. RF.3.3a and selected multisyllabic decoding work support word reading; L.3.4b–c support meaningful affix and base/root analysis. Discussion and explanatory writing are vehicles for making that learning visible.',
    'The selection preserves the difference between comprehension, viewpoint, word recognition, and word meaning.','Confirm current Grade 3 standards and the final task’s coverage.');
  m('B-scope-3','State the foundational and meaning-making goals clearly enough to assess.',
    'Foundational goal: students will accurately read and spell selected base-and-affix words, explain a taught part’s contribution to meaning, and apply the process with an appropriate known base in a new word. Meaning-making goal: students will state a main idea about beavers’ effects, explain two relevant details, distinguish a checkable claim from an evaluation, and accurately compare the author’s viewpoint with their own. I will score these components separately before judging the overall goals.',
    'Two explicit goals keep a strong comprehension response from hiding an unresolved word-reading need.','Planned assessment items and criteria for every selected goal.');
  m('B-scope-4','Select language standards that amplify the reading work.',
    'Grade 3 collaborative discussion, close reading, and explaining with reasons/evidence fit this unit. Possible Bridging references are ELD.PI.3.1.Br, ELD.PI.3.6.Br, and ELD.PI.3.11.Br. I will select the appropriate level and support from the actual learners’ language evidence; I will not assign the group Bridging merely because this sample names it. The language work supports both explaining word meanings and making text-based claims.',
    'It explains the standards’ purpose while keeping the proficiency decision evidence-based.','Actual language evidence and the selected Grade 3 ELD expectations.',C);
  m('B-scope-5','Write a language goal, not a list of scaffolds.',
    'Students will explain a word meaning or text interpretation with a relevant reason, accurately paraphrase another reader’s idea, and respond with a clarifying question or text-based contribution. Optional connectives and rehearsal support that performance but are not the goal. I will gather an individual explanation and evidence of each learner’s listening response rather than infer language learning from a completed shared paragraph.',
    'The goal is observable in both word analysis and comprehension discussion.','Individual explanation, paraphrase, and relevant response under stated support conditions.');
  m('B-scope-6','Specify a cumulative foundational-reading sequence.',
    'I will explicitly teach build/rebuild, then build/builder and help/helper/helpful, with retrieval and application across all three lessons. Students will inspect the spelling, read and blend the complete word, explain the meaningful part, spell selected words, and read them in sentences. Familiar free bases provide the initial root/base work. I will teach any necessary sound–spelling patterns and use context to confirm meaning, not to guess the printed word.',
    'The plan is direct, systematic, and explicit while integrating word reading and meaning.','Word list, model, guided practice, independent response, and connected-text use.');
  m('B-scope-7','Name an additional ELA/ELD Framework theme and show it across lessons.',
    'Meaning making is the additional theme across all three lessons. Students first connect details to a main idea in Text A, then examine claim type and author viewpoint in Text B, and finally compare information from Texts A and C with reduced support. The factual/evaluative distinction serves comprehension; it is not an isolated sorting exercise detached from what the texts say.',
    'The theme has a visible progression and a substantive reading purpose.','Main-idea explanations, claim justifications, and a comparison using text evidence.');
  m('B-scope-8','Summarize what happens in each of the three lessons.',
    'Lesson 1 introduces the beaver context, explicitly teaches build/rebuild, and develops a main idea from Text A through independent reading, paired explanation, and an individual exit response. Lesson 2 retrieves the prefix, teaches selected suffixes, and compares factual claims and evaluations in Texts A–B, using Text C to investigate “every.” Lesson 3 uses cumulative word practice and text comparison, then collects individual application before shared revision. The summative assessment follows lesson 3.',
    'The reader can see three distinct lessons and a separate summative assessment.','Three daily plans and the assessment described in C and supplied in D.');
  m('B-scope-9','Explain why the order builds learning, not simply that the topic stays the same.',
    'Students first learn to connect a detail to a general idea. They then use evidence to distinguish the kind of claim an author makes and whether it is supported. Finally, they apply these habits to a contrasting place and explain a qualified conclusion. Word instruction is cumulative: known bases support prefix learning, then suffix contrasts, then application. Language support moves from modeled explanation and paraphrase toward more independent use.',
    'Each lesson adds a demand and reuses a learned resource.','Connections between daily goals, checks, and the final assessment.');
  m('B-scope-10','Explain how the assessment will measure the goals you just stated.',
    'After lesson 3, one linked assessment will include an individual word-reading/meaning record, a short spelling check, a written main-idea and claim/viewpoint response, and a brief discussion performance. The criteria separately examine word reading and meaning, main idea and support, claim/viewpoint reasoning, and ELD explanation and listening response. I will document assistance and missing responses, and use the same criteria when analyzing the group and focus student.',
    'It provides an alignment between goals, tasks, and evidence rather than promising to assess “understanding.”','The blank task and teacher criteria in D, then actual responses and results after teaching.');

  const lessonExamples=[
    [
      'Students will read and explain build/rebuild and state a main idea for Text A with two relevant details and an explanation of their connection. I will distinguish a topic label such as “beavers” from a statement that accounts for the passage’s effects on wildlife and people.',
      'Students will explain how a detail supports a main idea and accurately restate a partner’s explanation before asking a relevant question. I will model a meaningful because connection, then accept other accurate ways to express the relationship.',
      'I will briefly clarify dam, pond, wetland, and airstrip; model build/rebuild; and read connected sentences. Students will independently mark details in Text A, explain a proposed main idea in pairs, compare ideas in the whole group, and write an individual main idea with two supporting details. They will identify what they reread or reconsidered.',
      'I will show the known base build, add re-, say and inspect the parts, blend the whole word, and explain “build again” in the sentence. Students will read, build, and spell the word, then practice with a suitable known base such as paint. I will listen to individual responses and correct a specific reading or meaning error before returning to text.',
      'For meaning making, I will model how a detail relates to an idea, then ask students to test competing main ideas against the whole passage. Partners will explain which detail supports which part of the idea. I will not provide the final main idea before learners have examined the text.',
      'I will model two kinds of explanation: how re- changes build, and how a detail supports a main idea. Students will rehearse orally, point to the relevant print, paraphrase a partner, and then write their own explanation. The language support is connected to the actual meaning, not a requirement to insert because somewhere.',
      'I will provide legible text, brief vocabulary clarification, a simple cause-and-effect representation, and processing time. An oral reading may support comprehension access when needed, but I will record it and collect independent print-reading evidence separately. The goal still requires learners to connect details to a main idea.',
      'Every student will mark a detail before discussion, explain a choice to a partner, listen and paraphrase, and produce an individual exit response. Reciprocal roles make both contributions visible. My questions will press students to explain why a detail belongs rather than reward only frequent speaking.',
      'If actual evidence shows a strength in explaining through diagrams, I will invite a diagram to support a text-based explanation. If a documented experience with changing water or animal habitats is relevant, I will use it to raise a question that students investigate in the text. I will not treat the connection as proof of the passage’s claims.',
      'I will check individual reading and meaning of rebuild, then collect a main-idea exit response with two details. I will note whether a student gives only a topic, selects unrelated details, or omits the explanation connecting a relevant detail. Those different responses will guide the next model or question.',
      'I will choose the FS adaptation from Part A evidence. If accurate oral comprehension contrasts with difficulty reading the printed word, I will provide targeted print instruction and record any read-aloud access. If word reading is sound but the detail-to-idea relationship is unclear, I will use a brief detail-sorting and explanation support instead. Neither profile is assumed in this example.'
    ],
    [
      'Students will read and explain builder, helper, and helpful using the taught bases and suffixes. They will distinguish a checkable claim from an evaluation, examine its support, and accurately compare Text B’s author viewpoint with their own. A false factual claim will not be relabeled an opinion merely because it is false.',
      'Students will justify a claim classification with a reason, accurately restate a different interpretation, and use evidence to clarify agreement or disagreement. They will distinguish “This can be checked” from “The text supports this,” because claim type and truth are different judgments.',
      'I will retrieve rebuild, explicitly teach the suffix examples, and read Text B alongside Text A. Students will first classify statements independently, then compare reasons in pairs. We will use Text C to investigate the claim about every beaver building a dam. Each student will write a comparison of the author’s and their own viewpoints.',
      'I will compare build/builder and help/helper/helpful, identify the meaningful suffix, read the complete word, explain the changed meaning, and guide spelling and sentence use. I will retrieve re- before adding the new material. I will use beaver as a nonexample: the ending letters do not establish a meaningful beav + -er split.',
      'For meaning making, students will examine what a sentence claims, whether it can be checked, and which evidence supports or challenges it. They will compare the evaluative “most fascinating” statement with the habitat claim and the overgeneralization about every beaver. They will then represent the author’s viewpoint accurately even if their own preference differs.',
      'I will model language for justification and qualification: a claim is checkable because evidence could confirm or contradict it; an evaluation expresses a judgment. Partners will use text references, paraphrase, and a relevant question. Optional prompts support both word-part explanations and claim reasoning, with support adjusted from actual language evidence.',
      'I will make the two texts easy to compare, provide time to reread, and allow students to point to evidence while explaining orally before writing. I will retain the required claim/viewpoint reasoning rather than substitute a simpler label-only task. I will record which supports the FS uses and the resulting response.',
      'Each learner will make an initial classification and justification before partner comparison. The listener must restate the reasoning, not just agree. Whole-group discussion will examine contrasting claims, and the final individual viewpoint response will show what each student understands after discussion.',
      'If students have a documented strength in comparing viewpoints during discussion, I will use that routine to examine Text B while requiring accurate evidence. Relevant student questions can help select which claim to investigate. I will not invent a class interest or assume that disagreement itself demonstrates comprehension.',
      'I will inspect a word-part explanation and the reason for classifying an unfamiliar statement. If a learner calls a false checkable claim an opinion, I will compare claim type with verification using the text. If the learner labels accurately but gives no reason, I will probe the basis rather than count the label as a complete response.',
      'I will adjust the FS support using actual lesson 1 evidence. A learner who identified a detail but did not explain its connection may benefit from a short oral evidence-to-claim rehearsal. A learner with continuing word-reading difficulty may need explicit print work alongside access to discussion. I will preserve the original response before collecting a supported revision.'
    ],
    [
      'Students will apply the taught word-analysis process in connected sentences and compare relevant information from Texts A and C. They will write an explanation supported by details and distinguish an unsupported generalization from an opinion. I will use a separate assessment after this lesson to judge the unit goals.',
      'Students will give a text-based reason, accurately paraphrase another interpretation, and respond with clarification or elaboration. I will reduce optional frames when the evidence supports doing so, while retaining necessary access supports. Independence means the learner supplies the meaning, not that all accommodations disappear.',
      'I will begin with brief cumulative word reading and spelling, then ask students to compare the beaver contexts in Texts A and C. Students will write an individual explanation before paired comparison and annotated revision. They will identify which reading or reasoning strategy helped and where. After lesson 3, I will administer the linked summative task with its planned conditions.',
      'I will retrieve re-, -er, and -ful with known bases, model any remaining print difficulty, and guide application in sentences. A new-base item is appropriate only if the base and relevant spellings are already known. I will collect an individual response before supplying a correction so the assessment record distinguishes independent from supported performance.',
      'Students will compare how beavers affect different places and explain why Text C challenges the universal dam claim. I will ask which details justify the comparison and what the texts leave uncertain. The goal is a supported, appropriately qualified interpretation rather than a collection of facts copied from the passage.',
      'I will ask students to connect ideas with precise comparison and reason language, refer to the source text, and paraphrase before responding. Brief oral planning can support the writing, but the initial independent product will be collected before partner revision. I will record individual discussion contributions rather than award language evidence to both partners from one shared paragraph.',
      'I will retain readable materials, processing time, and verified access accommodations. Students may point to their evidence while explaining. I will document read-aloud or other assistance because it changes the inference about independent reading; I will not remove required supports simply to make the recording look independent.',
      'Each learner will produce an individual comparison, then examine a partner’s reasoning and revise with a stated reason. A metacognitive explanation will name the strategy and its effect, such as rereading the riverbank sentence to reconsider “every.” The student need not change an accurate interpretation merely to demonstrate reflection.',
      'I will invite a documented strength or relevant question to support comparison across the texts, while requiring the conclusion to remain text-based. If the FS uses diagrams effectively, the learner can first organize a cause or contrast visually and then explain it. The actual asset must be identified in Part A, not invented here.',
      'Before the summative task I will check one word-analysis application and one text comparison. I will record the exact response and assistance, use the finding to decide whether a brief clarification is needed, and keep that formative record distinct from the later summative response. I will not silently change the scoring criteria after seeing answers.',
      'I will use the FS’s lesson 1–2 evidence to decide which support to retain, reduce, or change. If a detail-to-claim explanation becomes accurate after a prompt, I will check a new explanation with less optional prompting. If access support remains necessary, I will keep it and describe the conditions. The actual response—not this plan—will determine the judgment.'
    ]
  ];
  const means=['Specify the day’s ELA learning, connected to the unit goals.','State the day’s language performance, connected to the ELD unit goal.','Show the actual sequence and grouping, not only the topic.','Make the explicit model, cumulative practice, and individual check visible.','Explain how learners construct meaning in this lesson.','Show language development inside the reading and word work.','Connect an access option to the barrier and intended goal.','Ensure each learner thinks, communicates, listens, and produces evidence.','Use actual assets; a possible connection is not a verified profile.','Name what you will inspect and how different responses change instruction.','Choose and adjust the FS support from actual evidence.'];
  const reasons=['A daily goal gives the lesson and its evidence a common focus.','Language performance is more than a vocabulary list or supplied sentence frame.','The sequence connects explicit instruction, individual thinking, collaboration, and independent evidence.','The learner reads and applies the word, rather than guessing from context or only repeating a teacher.','The additional theme remains substantive and connected to the text.','The support develops the meaning learners need to express.','Access is planned without confusing supported performance with a different skill.','Participation is connected to learning evidence, not only activity completion.','The asset has an instructional purpose and must be documented.','Formative assessment becomes formative when its evidence informs a response.','The plan is conditional until the actual FS need and prior response are established.'];
  for(let l=1;l<=3;l++)for(let i=0;i<11;i++)m(`B-L${l}-${i+1}`,means[i],lessonExamples[l-1][i],reasons[i],i===8||i===10?'Actual Part A and previous lesson evidence; identify the support and intended recheck.':`Lesson ${l} task, learner response, assistance, and the relevant goal criterion.`,i===8||i===10?C:P);

  m('C-1','Restate the goals actually taught and assessed, without adding new targets.',
    'The assessment will evaluate the unit’s word-reading/analysis goal, its informational meaning-making goal, and its ELD explanation-and-response goal. Students will read and spell the selected word forms and explain the meaningful parts; state a main idea with relevant support and analyze claims/viewpoint; and give a reasoned explanation with an accurate listening response. These are the goals established in Part B, not additional goals introduced after teaching.',
    'The assessment is accountable to the planned learning.','Use the same wording and selected criteria across B, C, D, and I.');
  m('C-2','Explain what you will collect from each individual.',
    'I will retain each student’s written comprehension and viewpoint response, a brief teacher record of individual word reading/meaning and spelling, and an individual record of the planned discussion performance. I will identify assistance separately. A shared paragraph or a single group recording will not be treated as every student’s response. If an item is missing, I will mark it missing rather than infer a misconception.',
    'The records distinguish different skills and preserve individual attribution.','Blank record sheets in D; actual responses are collected after teaching.');
  m('C-3','Describe the actual assessment after the third lesson.',
    'After lesson 3, students will complete one linked assessment using Texts A–C. They will read selected words, explain a word part, complete a short spelling task, write a main idea supported by two details, classify and justify claims, and compare the author’s viewpoint with their own. A brief discussion will capture paraphrase and a text-based response. I will use the planned directions and access supports and collect the individual product before any peer revision.',
    'It gives a usable task and an administration sequence rather than “I will use a worksheet.”','Part D supplies the blank task and criterion guide.');
  m('C-4','Define what the evidence must show before judging results.',
    'I will look for accurate word reading, a valid base-and-affix explanation, and selected spelling; a main idea supported by two relevant connected details; separation of checkability from truth and an accurate viewpoint comparison; and an individual explanation with accurate paraphrase and relevant response. The criterion guide defines met, not yet, and exceeded before teaching. I will not let a polished comprehension answer conceal missing word-reading evidence or infer listening from turn-taking alone.',
    'Criteria make the learning judgment inspectable and keep the constructs distinct.','Apply the pre-established criterion guide, record missing evidence, and preserve the actual response.');

  const fExamples=[
    ['Locate the explicit foundational-skills teaching and explain its sequence.','In the proposed lesson, the strongest opportunity is the build → rebuild model followed by guided and individual application: inspect the letters, identify the meaningful parts, read the whole word, explain it, and use it in a sentence. After teaching, I would name the exact model and student response visible in my clip. A teacher explanation alone does not show that students learned to apply the process.','The proposed sequence identifies evidence to seek, not an outcome to report.','Explicit model, student application, feedback, and the subsequent response.'],
    ['Show students doing the foundational work, not only watching it.','I would seek footage where individual students read a complete word, construct or identify its meaningful parts, explain the change in meaning, and apply it in a sentence. Choral repetition can provide practice but can hide individual uncertainty. My commentary must identify what learners actually do and what their responses reveal, rather than describe the planned pocket-chart activity as proof of engagement or learning.','Engagement is tied to an observable literacy performance.','An individual word-reading/meaning response and its support condition.'],
    ['Explain how students worked on the additional theme of meaning making.','A useful moment is a student proposing a main idea or classifying a claim, followed by a question about the supporting detail. I would analyze whether the student connects the detail to the idea, not just whether the student points to a sentence. For a false factual claim, I would examine whether the learner distinguishes checkability from truth. The example unit supplies the planned opportunity, not a recorded response.','The analysis looks at the meaning constructed, not worksheet completion.','Actual text explanation, listener response, and teacher probe.'],
    ['Show how an actual asset was used while teaching the additional theme.','If a documented strength in visual explanation informed a cause-and-effect sketch, my commentary would identify the prior evidence, the use visible in the clip, and the student’s subsequent explanation. I would not write that all students were motivated by animals simply because the text concerned beavers. The effect must be supported by what the learners do with the text.','The claim connects an evidenced asset to teaching and learning.','Documented asset, actual move, and observed reading/reasoning response.'],
    ['Explain integrated ELD in the actual word and comprehension work.','I would identify how students were supported to explain an affix’s contribution or a text-based claim, then analyze their use of that language. For example, the clip might show a paraphrase, a relevant clarification, and a reason linked to a detail. Repeating “I think…because…” without supplying a valid reason would be participation in the routine, not sufficient evidence of the language goal.','The interpretation concerns meaningful language use, not the mere presence of a frame.','Actual explanation and listening response, with scaffolds identified.'],
    ['Describe the UDL option, its purpose, and the visible response.','If I provide a readable comparison of the two texts and time for oral rehearsal, I would identify the barrier those options address and the response that follows. If I read the passage aloud, the resulting explanation may show supported comprehension; it does not establish independent print reading. I would preserve that distinction in the impact field and use the separate word-reading record for that skill.','The strategy is linked to a barrier and a bounded interpretation of evidence.','Option actually used, response, and assistance conditions.'],
    ['Show how you interpreted a formative response against a goal.','I would identify the initial response: for example, a classification with its stated reason or a main idea with its selected details. Then I would explain which criterion the response met or left uncertain. Asking for a thumbs-up would not supply the same evidence as hearing why a false checkable claim remains factual in type. I must use the actual response, not the one anticipated in my plan.','Monitoring names the evidence and the criterion used to interpret it.','Actual response, learning criterion, and the next instructional decision.'],
    ['Explain the change or response you made because of that evidence.','If a learner calls a false claim an opinion, a planned response is to separate “Can it be checked?” from “Does the evidence support it?” and examine a contrasting statement. After teaching I would identify the response I actually made and analyze the learner’s next explanation. If the learner still confuses the categories, I should report the continuing need; giving corrective feedback does not by itself establish progress.','The evidence chain includes the response to teaching and permits a limited or unsuccessful result.','Initial response → teacher decision → actual next response, at verified clip times.']
  ];
  fExamples.forEach((a,i)=>m('F'+(i+1),...a,C));

  m('I1','Review how activities and formative checks supported progress across the actual three lessons.',
    'I would trace one goal through the sequence: the initial main idea in lesson 1, the evidence-to-claim explanation in lesson 2, and the individual comparison in lesson 3. For word work, I would compare reading and meaning across the cumulative sequence. The analysis should identify which teaching response followed each finding and what learners then demonstrated. The proposed beaver unit has not been taught in the supplied record, so it provides no before-and-after results to report.',
    'A sequence of evidence and decisions is more informative than a list of activities.','Actual lesson and formative records, with dates, responses, and assistance.',R);
  m('I2','List the assessed goals and enter actual counts in the official table.',
    'The table must summarize the actual ELA/literacy and ELD judgments under the criteria established in C–D. I would record how many students were assessed, identify unavailable responses, and check that my categories account for the assessed group. I would retain component-level records for word reading and meaning making so one strength does not conceal another need. No counts or scores can be supplied for this untaught model unit.',
    'Blank or unavailable evidence is not a zero, and a class average does not replace goal-level counts.','Actual scored criteria, denominators, missing-work notes, and the goal-level decision rule.',R);
  m('I3-group-ela','Analyze group patterns in literacy, not only an overall total.',
    'A useful analysis would distinguish accurate word reading from affix meaning, and a topic label from a main idea supported by connected details. If responses contain a relevant detail but omit its relation to the idea, that is a more specific pattern than “comprehension was weak.” I would show actual examples and counts where appropriate and identify whether the responses were independent or supported. These are questions to investigate, not claimed results of this unit.',
    'The pattern leads to a teachable relationship rather than a general ability label.','Actual group responses and criterion-level records.',C);
  m('I3-group-eld','Analyze the language goal using language evidence.',
    'I would examine individual explanations, paraphrases, and responses to another reader. A group may produce many speaking turns without showing accurate listening or a text-linked reason. Conversely, a concise contribution can provide strong evidence if it accurately takes up a partner’s idea and supports a claim. I would report the actual pattern and assistance rather than infer ELD achievement from the comprehension worksheet alone.',
    'The evidence matches the language performance that was planned.','Actual discussion or explanation records for individual students.',C);
  m('I3-fs-ela','Analyze the focus student’s literacy components separately.',
    'For the FS I would compare the actual word-reading/meaning record and comprehension response. A strong explanation after a read-aloud can establish comprehension under that condition while leaving independent print reading uncertain. If the written response names only “beavers,” I would inspect whether oral reasoning shows a broader idea before selecting the next support. The evidence must come from the FS, not a group product or historical sample.',
    'A within-student comparison can locate a specific need without flattening the learner.','Actual FS work, oral response, criteria, and assistance.',C);
  m('I3-fs-eld','Analyze the FS’s explanation and response to another person.',
    'I would identify exactly what the FS said or wrote, which relationship was communicated, and how the student took up another reader’s idea. A correctly repeated frame does not establish a meaningful reason. If a prompt was needed, I would describe supported performance and seek a less-supported response before claiming independent use. The current case supplies no actual FS discussion or ELPAC result.',
    'The conclusion distinguishes meaningful language use, prompting, and independence.','Individual FS explanation, paraphrase, response, and supports.',C);
  m('I4-group','Explain what the instructional approach appears to have contributed, with limits.',
    'If actual responses improve after comparing too-narrow and appropriately broad main ideas, I would identify the before-and-after work and explain why the contrast may have helped. I would also consider whether repeated text exposure, partner assistance, or teacher wording accounts for part of the change. I would not claim that the strategy caused improvement solely because later responses were stronger.',
    'The interpretation connects instruction to evidence without overstating causation.','Actual teaching sequence, responses, conditions, and plausible alternatives.',C);
  m('I4-fs','Evaluate the specific effect of instruction for the FS.',
    'I would compare what the FS did before the targeted support, after it, and on a new check. If the learner explains a main idea accurately only while following my model, the support may have enabled participation without yet establishing independent application. That finding can justify further instruction. It is more useful than calling the strategy effective because the student completed the task.',
    'The impact judgment is specific to the learner and the level of support.','FS before/support/after evidence and any independent transfer check.',C);
  m('I5-group','Propose a specific instructional change justified by the actual group pattern.',
    'If the group’s evidence shows confusion between checkability and truth, I would add an earlier contrast among a supported factual claim, a contradicted factual claim, and an evaluation. Students would justify two separate decisions before discussing viewpoint. If the main need is instead word reading, I would change the explicit word instruction rather than add more claim sorting. The chosen change must follow the actual results.',
    'It changes the relevant teaching demand rather than promising more time or more practice without a target.','Actual pattern, changed teaching move, and a planned recheck.',C);
  m('I5-fs','Choose a change that responds to the FS’s demonstrated need.',
    'If the FS can select a relevant detail but not explain its connection, I would model that connection briefly, guide a second example, and ask for a new independent explanation. I would retain any required access supports and reduce optional prompts only when appropriate. If the actual evidence reveals a decoding need, the revision would instead include targeted print instruction while preserving access to meaning making.',
    'The change is targeted and includes a way to judge whether it helped.','FS criterion-level evidence, selected change, and new response opportunity.',C);
  m('I6','Describe the specific feedback actually provided to the FS.',
    'For a response with a relevant but unconnected detail, a proposed feedback move is: “This detail belongs in the passage. Show which part of your main idea it supports and explain the connection. Then check your second detail.” In Part I I must describe the feedback I actually gave, including the correct detail or need it addressed, and make sure it matches Part H. This proposed wording is not evidence that feedback occurred.',
    'It identifies a strength, a next action, and the exact evidence relationship to repair.','Actual feedback from H and the original FS response.',C);
  m('I7','Explain how the feedback supports progress without inventing evidence that it was used.',
    'The proposed detail-to-idea feedback focuses the learner on the missing relationship rather than simply requesting a longer answer. I would give the FS an opportunity to revise and then explain a new detail. If I have the revision, I can analyze what changed; if not, I can explain the intended next learning without claiming that feedback was effective. Feedback provided and feedback used are different evidence claims.',
    'The explanation separates instructional purpose from a demonstrated outcome.','Feedback, any actual revision, and the next task or goal connection.',C);

  m('J1','Choose re-teaching or extension from the FS’s actual results.',
    'If the FS labels “Every beaver builds a dam” an opinion because it is false, I would choose re-teaching focused on separating claim type from verification. The decision would cite that response and the relevant formative evidence. If the learner already distinguishes these ideas and explains them with evidence, I would instead extend to comparing how two sources qualify a claim. Neither branch is a diagnosed need until supported by the actual FS record.',
    'The follow-up is selected because of evidence, not prepared as the same fourth lesson for every learner.','Specific findings from I and supporting Steps 1–2 evidence.',C);
  m('J2-ela','Name the focused literacy learning for the selected follow-up.',
    'For the re-teaching branch, the FS will distinguish a checkable claim from an evaluation and explain whether a short text supports, contradicts, or leaves the checkable claim unresolved. The learner will apply the distinction to a new statement rather than repeat the original classification. This is a focused continuation of the unit’s meaning-making goal.',
    'The goal narrows the next learning and requires transfer.','Use this branch only when the actual need warrants it.',C);
  m('J2-eld','State the language performance needed for that follow-up.',
    'The FS will explain the two decisions with a relevant reason and respond to a clarifying question: whether the statement can be checked, and what the available evidence says about it. Optional because or however prompts can support the relationship. The goal is the meaning communicated, not compulsory use of one sentence pattern.',
    'The language goal amplifies the selected literacy work.','An actual explanation and response, with the support level documented.',C);
  m('J3','Describe the targeted teaching, practice, feedback, and new check.',
    'I would display three brief statements: a text-supported checkable claim, a checkable claim contradicted by the text, and an evaluation. I would model the two decisions for one statement, guide the FS through another, and ask the learner to justify the third. I would give feedback on the reasoning, then present a new short paragraph and statement for independent application. For extension, I would instead compare two sources’ emphases and ask for an evidence-based qualification.',
    'The instruction addresses the identified relationship and includes a new opportunity to demonstrate it.','Select one evidence-supported branch, then record what actually happens.',C);
  m('J4','Use an actual FS asset to make the follow-up productive.',
    'If the FS has demonstrated strength in oral explanation, I would begin with a spoken reason and ask the learner to connect it to a written evidence note. If the documented strength is visual organization, I would use a two-column checkability/evidence organizer before the explanation. I would preserve the same reasoning target and identify the actual asset from Part A or the unit evidence.',
    'The asset changes the instructional entry point without replacing the goal.','Actual FS asset and how it supports the selected follow-up.',C);
  m('L-reteach','Choose this prompt only when the taught follow-up was a re-teaching activity.',
    'After teaching, I would identify the targeted distinction in my actual Part K clip, describe the model or question I used, and analyze the FS’s response with the assistance stated. If the learner still calls a false claim an opinion, I would explain the continuing difficulty and what the response suggests about my explanation. I would not turn an unsuccessful check into a success story or treat my proposed Part J plan as evidence that instruction occurred.',
    'The commentary links the earlier finding, actual teaching, and actual response—even when progress is limited.','Verified Part K timestamp, what/why/impact, and the specific goal.',R);
  m('L-extension','Choose this prompt only when the taught follow-up was an extension activity.',
    'After an actual extension, I would identify how comparing sources deepened the FS’s literacy work beyond the unit target. I would describe a response showing whether the learner considered both sources and qualified a conclusion, rather than report that the extra task was completed. If the learner needs support to use both sources, I would state that condition and the remaining need. The extension must follow real evidence of readiness.',
    'The commentary concerns deeper learning and its evidence, not merely additional work.','Actual Part K event and response; do not answer both L alternatives as if both were required.',R);
})();
